{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"evaluate-retrieval-quality","kind":"prompt","title":"Evaluate retrieval quality","description":"Builds a labelled query set and a script that measures a search or RAG retriever's recall@k, MRR and nDCG separately from answer quality, with per-query-type breakdowns and failure lists.","category":"ai-ml","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["verify"],"role":["ml-engineer","data-scientist","backend-engineer","software-engineer"],"stack":["llm-apps"],"requires":["repo-read","file-write","shell"],"inputs":["repo","dataset"],"output":["code","report","table"],"risk":"runs-commands","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["retrieval-evaluation","recall-at-k","information-retrieval","golden-set"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["design-rag-pipeline","write-llm-eval-suite","generate-synthetic-test-data"]},"args":[{"name":"retriever","description":"The retrieval code or endpoint to evaluate, the corpus, and what k the generator receives.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"query_sources","description":"Where real queries can come from (search logs, chat logs, support tickets, expert-written questions) and any existing labels.","type":"text","required":true}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Query set","Labelling","Metrics","Code","Baseline results","How to use it"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nWhen RAG answers are wrong, teams tweak the prompt, but most failures happen earlier: the right passage was never retrieved. Measuring retrieval on its own, with a labelled set of queries and their relevant chunks, shows whether a change to chunking, embeddings, hybrid search or reranking actually helped. The set must look like real traffic (short keyword queries, long questions, typos, identifiers, other languages) and include queries with no answer in the corpus. Labels tied to chunk ids break when chunking changes, so label at document or section level, or keep a mapping. Questions generated by an LLM from the passages themselves are easy (they reuse the passage's words) and inflate scores; use them only to top up real queries, and mark them.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a retrieval evaluation for: {{retriever}}\n\nQuery sources: {{query_sources}}\n\n1. Read the retriever code and how results are identified (chunk ids, document ids, sections).\n2. Query set: collect 50 to 300 queries, mostly real, stratified by type (lookup, identifier, paraphrase, multi-part, multilingual or typo, unanswerable). Mark synthetic ones. Remove personal data from logged queries.\n3. Labelling: for each query, the relevant documents or sections (graded if useful: highly relevant, partly relevant), at a level that survives re-chunking. Describe the labelling guide and, if possible, have two people label a sample and report agreement. Unanswerable queries have no relevant items.\n4. Metrics: recall@k at the k the generator receives and at a larger k, MRR, and nDCG if labels are graded; for unanswerable queries, the top score distribution, to help set a not-found threshold. Report overall and per query type.\n5. Write the evaluation script: load the query set, call the retriever, compute metrics, write a per-query results file listing misses with the retrieved items, and compare against a saved baseline run.\n6. Run it on the current retriever and report baseline numbers and the worst query types.\n7. Explain how to use it: run on every retrieval change, the minimum improvement that counts given the set size, and how to keep the set fresh with new real queries.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Keep retrieval metrics separate from answer metrics; do not use answer correctness as a proxy for retrieval.\n- State how small sample sizes limit conclusions (a few queries changing flips a percentage point).\n- Do not label using the retriever's own outputs alone; that bakes in its blind spots.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Query set\nTable: type | count | source | example.\n## Labelling\nThe guide, the label level and agreement results or plan.\n## Metrics\nDefinitions and the k values used.\n## Code\nCode blocks with file paths.\n## Baseline results\nReal numbers per query type, or a plain statement that it was not run.\n## How to use it\nWhen to run, thresholds and upkeep.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
